<h1>Adding configuration parameter</h1>
<p>Now that we have the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/maze-application/">Maze application</a> downloaded,
  let's write a test that runs it. This test introduces its first configuration parameter - a
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#filesystem-types"><code>Folder</code></a> field pointing at the
  folder holding the Maze executable.</p>

<h2 id="folder-parameter-test">Folder Parameter test</h2>
<p>This test lives in the tutorial suite's <strong>Basic Configuration Parameters</strong> test group, alongside
  the other parameter tests we'll cover in this and the next few chapters:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/adding-configuration-parameter/folder-parameter-test-gui.png", "Folder Parameter test selected under the Basic Configuration Parameters test group in the Orcraft controller's Test Explorer") }}
<p>Let's review the code of the file named <code>test_folder_parameter.py</code>, residing in this path:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_basic_configuration_parameters/test_folder_parameter/") }}

<h2 id="imports">Imports</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTest,
	OrcraftTestConfig,
	OrcraftTestCase,
	OrcraftConfigField,
)

from common.maze_app import MazeApp') }}
<p>Alongside the same framework building blocks used by the <a class="text-accent"
  href="/tutorial/hello-world-test-structure/#imports">Hello World test</a>, this test also imports
  <code>MazeApp</code> - a small helper, shared across the tutorial suite, that locates the Maze executable in a
  folder and runs it, logging its output.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/common/maze_app.py") }}

<h2 id="config-class">Config class</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestFolderParameterConfig(OrcraftTestConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Folder Parameter")

	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(
		default=(
			"Demonstrates a folder configuration parameter.\\n"
			"Runs the maze app executable with no arguments to display its help page and logs the output."
		)
	)
	
	maze_executable_folder = OrcraftConfigField.Folder(default="", label="Maze Executable Folder", required=True)') }}
<p><code>maze_executable_folder</code> is this test's first configuration parameter beyond the built-in
  <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>. It's declared with
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#filesystem-types"><code>OrcraftConfigField.Folder</code></a>, which
  renders as a folder picker in the controller's GUI.</p>
<p><code>label</code> sets the field's display name in the GUI, separate from its Python attribute name.
  <code>required=True</code> means the test can't be run until a folder is selected - there's no meaningful
  default, since it depends entirely on where you downloaded the Maze application to. These and the other
  options every field accepts are listed in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#options">Common config field options</a>.</p>

<h2 id="test-class">Test class</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestFolderParameter(OrcraftTest):
	def __init__(self, config: TestFolderParameterConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or TestFolderParameterConfig())

	def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

		maze_app = MazeApp(self.config.maze_executable_folder)

		maze_app.run()

		return case.result') }}
<p><code>_run_case</code> reads <code>self.config.maze_executable_folder</code> - the folder selected in the
  GUI (or the CLI), exposed as an ordinary attribute of the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/test/#config-class">config</a> - and passes it to <code>MazeApp</code>. Called with no command, <code>MazeApp</code> runs the
  executable with no arguments, which - as we saw in the <a class="text-accent"
  href="/tutorial/maze-application/#usage">Maze application</a> chapter - prints its help page.</p>

<h2 id="main">Main</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('def main() -> None:
	test = TestFolderParameter()
	test.run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}
